"(PDF) Everyday Climates: Household Archaeologies and the Politics of Scale (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392725253_Everyday_Climates_Household_Archaeologies_and_the_Politics_of_Scale)\nciteturn16view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 10 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392725253_Everyday_Climates_Household_Archaeologies_and_the_Politics_of_Scale\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2844\nL0: Article PDF Available\nL1: # Everyday Climates: Household Archaeologies and the Politics of Scale\nL2: \nL3: cite0† Heritage L4: \nL5:   * June 2025\nL6:   * 8(6):227\nL7: \nL8: DOI:cite1†10.3390/heritage8060227†doi.org L9: \nL10:   * License\nL11:   * cite2†CC BY 4.0 L12: \nL13: Authors:\nL14: \nL15: cite3†  L16: \nL17: cite3†Catherine Kearns L18: \nL19: cite3†Catherine Kearns L20: \nL21:   * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\nL22: \nL23: cite65†Image†www.researchgate.net L24: \nL25: cite4†Download full-text PDF cite5†Read full-text L26: \nL27: cite6†Download full-text PDF L28: \nL29: cite5†Read full-text L30: \nL31: cite7†Download citation L32: \nL33: Copy link Link copied\nL34: \nL35: * * *\nL36: cite5† Read full-text cite7† Download citation L37: \nL38: Copy link Link copied\nL39: \nL40: cite8†Citations (1) cite9†References (173) cite10†Figures (1) L41: ## Abstract and Figures\nL42: The small scale is recognized as a necessary rebuttal to macroscalar narratives of climate–society relationships in the past, and archeologists and historians have increasingly turned to advocating smaller and shorter scales of analysis and interpretation, from “microclimates” to interannual droughts and single settlement histories.\nL43: Such provocations rightly caution against the dangers of oversimplification and determinism in recent planetary or Earth-systems approaches to human history, as well as push scholars to acknowledge human-scale experiences: weather, seasonality, landscape change. When it comes to smaller-scale remains, however, like those of household practices, we often consider them data or proxies that validate larger-scale arguments about societal persistence or economic vulnerability.\nL44: Yet the material and ideational ways that people in premodern worlds made sense of their surroundings, especially via gendered and class-based rhythms of production and consumption, were deeply entwined in the politics of everyday household life. What would a household archeology of climate entail? In this paper I highlight how households themselves were critical sites of environmental construction, experience and history-making through a selection of examples of archeological work from the Mediterranean.\nL45: I argue that archeologists can critically rethink themes of persistence and adaptation by taking seriously the scalar constructions and varied politics of domestic life.\nL46: cite11†  L47: \nL48: cite11†Sites mentioned in text. Map created by author. …  L49: \nL50: Figures - available from: cite0†Heritage L51: \nL52: This content is subject to copyright.\nL53: \nL54: cite66†Image: ResearchGate Logo L55: \nL56: Discover the world's research\nL57: \nL58:   * 25+ million members\nL59:   * 160+ million publication pages\nL60:   * 2.3+ billion citations\nL61: \nL62: cite12†Join for free L63: \nL64: Publisher Full-text 1\nL65: \nL66: Access to this full-text is provided by MDPI.\nL67: \nL68: cite13†Learn more†help.researchgate.net L69: \nL70: Content available from cite0†Heritage L71: \nL72: This content is subject to copyright.\nL73: Academic Editors: Sharon Steadman\nL74: \nL75: and John Frederick Haldon\nL76: \nL77: Received: 15 April 2025\nL78: \nL79: Revised: 30 May 2025\nL80: \nL81: Accepted: 10 June 2025\nL82: \nL83: Published: 14 June 2025\nL84: \nL85: Citation: Kearns, C. Everyday\nL86: \nL87: Climates: Household Archaeologies\nL88: \nL89: and the Politics of Scale. Heritage 2025,\nL90: \nL91: 8, 227. https://doi.org/10.3390/\nL92: \nL93: heritage8060227\nL94: \nL95: Copyright: © 2025 by the author.\nL96: \nL97: Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland.\nL98: \nL99: This article is an open access article\nL100: \nL101: distributed under the terms and\nL102: \nL103: conditions of the Creative Commons\nL104: Attribution (CC BY) license\nL105: \nL106: (https://creativecommons.org/\nL107: \nL108: licenses/by/4.0/).\nL109: \nL110: Article\nL111: \nL112: Everyday Climates: Household Archaeologies and the Politics\nL113: \nL114: of Scale\nL115: \nL116: Catherine Kearns\nL117: \nL118: Department of Classics, University of Chicago, Chicago, IL 60637, USA; cmkearns@uchicago.edu\nL119: \nL120: Abstract: The small scale is recognized as a necessary rebuttal to macroscalar narratives\nL121: \nL122: of climate–society relationships in the past, and archeologists and historians have increas-\nL123: ingly turned to advocating smaller and shorter scales of analysis and interpretation, from\nL124: \nL125: “microclimates” to interannual droughts and single settlement histories. Such provoca-\nL126: \nL127: tions rightly caution against the dangers of oversimpliﬁcation and determinism in recent\nL128: \nL129: planetary or Earth-systems approaches to human history, as well as push scholars to ac-\nL130: \nL131: knowledge human-scale experiences: weather, seasonality, landscape change. When it\nL132: comes to smaller-scale remains, however, like those of household practices, we often con-\nL133: \nL134: sider them data or proxies that validate larger-scale arguments about societal persistence\nL135: \nL136: or economic vulnerability. Y et the material and ideational ways that people in premod-\nL137: \nL138: ern worlds made sense of their surroundings, especially via gendered and class-based\nL139: \nL140: rhythms of production and consumption, were deeply entwined in the politics of everyday\nL141: household life. What would a household archeology of climate entail? In this paper I\nL142: \nL143: highlight how households themselves were critical sites of environmental construction,\nL144: \nL145: experience and history-making through a selection of examples of archeological work\nL146: \nL147: from the Mediterranean. I argue that archeologists can critically rethink themes of persis-\nL148: \nL149: tence and adaptation by taking seriously the scalar constructions and varied politics of\nL150: \nL151: domestic life.\nL152: Keywords: scale; household archeology; Mediterranean; everyday; climate\nL153: \nL154: 1. Introduction\nL155: \nL156: Climate change feels big. W e recognize, to begin, that climate is a complex process\nL157: \nL158: whose interlocking dynamics and variables of atmospheric, oceanic, terrestrial and biotic\nL159: \nL160: components operate at large scales. While we often acknowledge that humans primarily\nL161: \nL162: experience climatic changes through weather, by perceiving transitions in expected norms\nL163: and intimately engaging with shifting conditions of temperature or precipitation, we\nL164: \nL165: naturally turn to the bigger, regional and even global idea of climate to make sense of\nL166: \nL167: things [\nL168: \nL169: 1\nL170: \nL171: ,\nL172: \nL173: 2\nL174: \nL175: ]. When we then move to study premodern environments, that big scale, both\nL176: \nL177: temporal and spatial, seems ﬁtting and even necessary, given the often coarse and widely\nL178: \nL179: varying records we have to reconstruct and interpr et past environments. As archeologists,\nL180: we speak most conﬁdently in terms of societal relationships to climate, through structural\nL181: \nL182: forces like subsistence economies, institutions, migration practices, settlements or cultural\nL183: \nL184: ideas of nature. We tend to seek out diachronic patterns and r egional variations in evidence\nL185: \nL186: that can provide comparative information on the impacts of climatic or environmental\nL187: \nL188: changes and how human societies might have responded differently across time and space.\nL189: A big-scale approach to premodern environmental or climatic history has therefor e\nL190: \nL191: stuck, as historians and archeologists have turned to examining ancient human–climate\nL192: \nL193: Heritage 2025,8, 227 https://doi.org/10.3390/heritage8060227\nL194: \nL195: Heritage 2025,8, 227 2 of 20\nL196: \nL197: interactions and to the recognition that environmental records ar e key to more robust\nL198: \nL199: interpretations of historical transformation [\nL200: \nL201: 3\nL202: \nL203: ,\nL204: \nL205: 4\nL206: \nL207: ]. Shifts in historiographical conversations\nL208: lend support to this larger focus—globalizing, deep or even planetary history has gained\nL209: \nL210: standing for its ability to synchronize varying complicated sets of data to narrate diachronic\nL211: \nL212: processes of societal growth and decline, topics clearly, even obviously, relevant today [\nL213: \nL214: 5\nL215: \nL216: ].\nL217: \nL218: In new ﬁelds like the “history of climate and society,” for example, scholars advocate analy-\nL219: \nL220: ses that integrate multiple kinds of natural proxies and historical and material evidence to\nL221: reveal the complexities of past responses to and interventions in environmental changes [\nL222: \nL223: 6\nL224: \nL225: ].\nL226: \nL227: The uneven resolution of many natural records, as well as the challenges of seeking out\nL228: \nL229: signiﬁcant patterns from complex background noise, often attenuate our focus to centuries-\nL230: \nL231: long or millennia-long transformations. Despite these issues, these studies are clearly\nL232: \nL233: attractive for their apparent complexity in analyzing coupled human–nature systems [\nL234: \nL235: 7\nL236: \nL237: ,\nL238: \nL239: 8\nL240: \nL241: ].\nL242: In that vein, systems theory is re-emerging in archeological studies of societal persistence\nL243: \nL244: and adaptation, as recent work in resilience and sustainability studies shows [\nL245: \nL246: 9\nL247: \nL248: –\nL249: \nL250: 11\nL251: \nL252: ]. Schol-\nL253: \nL254: ars acknowledge the risks of thinking big in recent planetary or Earth-systems approaches\nL255: \nL256: to human history—reductionism and overgeneralization, even determinism—but advocate\nL257: \nL258: the heuristic power of these frameworks, based on the records we have, e.g., [\nL259: \nL260: 4\nL261: \nL262: ]. Climate\nL263: change in the past, then, can still feel big.\nL264: \nL265: Efforts to descend beneath graphs of global atmospheric concentrations of carbon or\nL266: \nL267: regional surveys of data to think about people’s real, lived or even imagined experiences\nL268: \nL269: with climatic or environmental change would seem, then, important to complementing this\nL270: \nL271: macroscalar analytical bent ([\nL272: \nL273: 12\nL274: \nL275: ,\nL276: \nL277: 13\nL278: \nL279: ], see also [\nL280: \nL281: 2\nL282: \nL283: ]). Archeologists have certainly called out the\nL284: poor resolution of some proxies, as opposed to records such as tr ee rings or speleothems,\nL285: \nL286: as well as the lack of people in many big environmental histories, the multiple and often in-\nL287: \nL288: commensurate scales of practice that forged human–climate relationships and the nuanced,\nL289: \nL290: nonlinear realities of the impacts of droughts, ﬂoods or other phenomena on premodern\nL291: \nL292: life [\nL293: \nL294: 13\nL295: \nL296: –\nL297: \nL298: 15\nL299: \nL300: ]. For the ancient Mediterranean, for example, we are increasingly more aware\nL301: of modeling the effects of acute droughts of 2–3 years on rainfed agriculture, as opposed\nL302: \nL303: to the potential longer-term impacts of regional trends in aridity [\nL304: \nL305: 16\nL306: \nL307: ]. Often, attempts to\nL308: \nL309: integrate multiscalar investigations of human–environment relationships circle around the\nL310: \nL311: measure of social hierarchies and macropolitical change—ruling elites or state institutions,\nL312: \nL313: on one side, and masses of populations on the other [\nL314: \nL315: 17\nL316: \nL317: ,\nL318: \nL319: 18\nL320: \nL321: ]. It is more challenging, based\nL322: on the available evidence and methods, to reach other scales of environmental and climatic\nL323: \nL324: histories: villages, communities, household networks and families [19].\nL325: \nL326: Y et the household was the center of life in premodern and preindustrial sedentary soci-\nL327: \nL328: eties throughout the Mediterranean, the regional focus of this paper. Indeed, up to the onset\nL329: \nL330: of industrialization, the household unit—typically deﬁned as a kin-based, co-residential\nL331: collective but not necessarily a nuclear family, given that their construction was historically\nL332: \nL333: and culturally contingent—was a crucially important site of production, consumption\nL334: \nL335: and the management of energy ﬂows between people and larger collectives [\nL336: \nL337: 20\nL338: \nL339: ,\nL340: \nL341: 21\nL342: \nL343: ]. It is\nL344: \nL345: for this reason, for example, that archeologists turn to long-term comparative analyses\nL346: \nL347: of household size to study phenomena like inequality from the past to the present [\nL348: \nL349: 22\nL350: \nL351: ].\nL352: Beyond resource or wealth accumulation, where people lived and worked conditioned their\nL353: \nL354: understandings, perceptions and imaginations of landscapes, environments and senses of\nL355: \nL356: climate [\nL357: \nL358: 12\nL359: \nL360: ,\nL361: \nL362: 23\nL363: \nL364: ]. Even with Western industrialization during the 18th and 19th centuries\nL365: \nL366: CE, as recent work is showing, relationships between energy consumption and climate\nL367: \nL368: change can be traced to domestic needs around coal and the choices and decisions that\nL369: changed daily life, directed economic strategies and redesigned the home [\nL370: \nL371: 24\nL372: \nL373: ]. It seems\nL374: \nL375: important to forward an archeology of the household-scale practices that mediated peo-\nL376: \nL377: ple’s experiences of shifting conditions through their norms, ideas and decisions relating\nL378: \nL379: Heritage 2025,8, 227 3 of 20\nL380: \nL381: to resources, landscapes, weather and climate. In other words, to think critically about\nL382: \nL383: how past people “domesticated” their environments, by examining how they established\nL384: ideas and expectations of climate through everyday activities and understandings of social\nL385: \nL386: orders operating at shorter rhythms and place-based scales [\nL387: \nL388: 1\nL389: \nL390: ,\nL391: \nL392: 25\nL393: \nL394: ,\nL395: \nL396: 26\nL397: \nL398: ]. I argue this not to\nL399: \nL400: rebalance our macroscalar investigations by prioritizing only the microscale as a way to\nL401: \nL402: explain historical change, but to advocate the study of mundane and quotidian climates\nL403: \nL404: through the tempos and spatialities of domestic life. In the Mediterranean, households and\n"
